<%#
  THE PROJECT FOLDER'S OWN ROOT FILES (stage 3, S3-4; narrowed 2026-09-08 by folder panels). This
  panel WAS the whole folder, section by section; each depth-one subfolder is now a panel of its
  own (public/projects/_folder_panel) and this one keeps the ROOT — the README, the loose files a
  visitor meets first (spec decision 4). `pub.loose_rows` is that list, name-ordered, with backups
  and dot-files already gone: a public page is not a recovery tool, and Node.visible_entries is the
  same display rule the owner's own page applies.

  ITS FOUR-CELL ROW IS ALSO _folder_panel's, deliberately in two places rather than a third
  partial: each copy sits in the panel that renders it, and what must not drift between them — the
  thumb's `url:`, the name's view URL, the download's `download: true` — is asserted on both panels
  in public_project_test.rb. A third caller is when to extract it.

  NO `data-controller="thumbs"` AND THAT IS DELIBERATE, not an omission. The poll exists to turn a
  spinner into an image while the owner watches their own upload render; a visitor has no such
  expectation and the endpoint it polls is signed-in-only anyway. So this renders READY thumbs and,
  for anything else, the spinner markup `files/_thumb` emits with its animation stopped
  (pages/public.css) — a static badge rather than a promise the page will never keep.

  THE NAME OPENS THE FILE'S OWN PAGE SINCE STAGE 4, S4-5; DOWNLOAD IS UNCHANGED. Until then both
  cells pointed at the BYTES, so clicking an .stl downloaded it and there was nowhere on the public
  side to look at it — which is what S4-5 gives a visitor: the same 3D viewer, Measure view, Gerber
  stage and KiCanvas embed the maker sees, at `pub.view_url_for`. The two cells now answer two
  different questions (show me this / give me this) rather than one question twice.

  Local: pub (Maker::PublicProject).
%>
<% rows = pub.loose_rows %>
<% if rows.any? %>
  <section class="panel files-panel">
    <div class="panel-head">
      <h2><%= panel_title(pub.layout, "attachments") %></h2>
      <span class="mono muted"><%= pluralize(rows.size, "file") %></span>
    </div>
    <%= panel_intro(pub.layout, "attachments") %>

    <table class="table public-files">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th class="col-thumb"></th>
          <th class="col-name">File</th>
          <th class="col-size">Size</th>
          <th class="col-dl"></th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <% rows.each do |row| %>
          <% ext = File.extname(row.node.name).delete(".").upcase %>
          <tr>
            <td class="col-thumb">
              <span class="file-cell">
                <%= render "files/thumb", thumb: pub.thumbs.thumb(row.node_id), ext_label: ext,
                           url: pub.preview_url_for(row.node_id) %>
              </span>
            </td>
            <%# The file's own PAGE (stage 4, S4-5), not its bytes — the header above says why.
                The Download cell below is where FileDelivery still decides inline-vs-attachment,
                exactly as it does for the owner's own /files/:id/blob. %>
            <td class="col-name"><%= link_to row.node.name, pub.view_url_for(row.node_id) %></td>
            <td class="col-size num"><%= number_to_human_size(row.node.size) %></td>
            <td class="col-dl">
              <%= link_to "Download", pub.file_url_for(row.node_id, download: true), class: "linkbtn small" %>
            </td>
          </tr>
        <% end %>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>
<% end %>
